    <node id="Why AI Harms Can't Be Fixed One Identity at a Time">
      <data key="d0">Why AI Harms Can't Be Fixed One Identity at a Time</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The paper "Why AI Harms Can't Be Fixed One Identity at a Time" discusses the limitations of current AI risk assessments in capturing intersectional harms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-381164f8e5eeb1538b53d25d3cf781b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779145987</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="5300 Incident Reports">
      <data key="d0">5300 Incident Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">The dataset consists of 5,300 reports from documented AI incidents, used to analyze AI harms and their intersectionality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-381164f8e5eeb1538b53d25d3cf781b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779145987</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AI Risk Assessment">
      <data key="d0">AI Risk Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">AI risk assessment is a tool for identifying harms caused by AI systems, but it often focuses on isolated identity categories rather than intersections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-381164f8e5eeb1538b53d25d3cf781b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779145994</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Intersectionality">
      <data key="d0">Intersectionality</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Intersectionality refers to the interaction between different identity categories (e.g., class and skin tone) that can produce unique harms in AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-381164f8e5eeb1538b53d25d3cf781b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779145994</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AI Incident Database">
      <data key="d0">AI Incident Database</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">The AI Incident Database is a curated source of incident data, used for analyzing documented AI incidents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-381164f8e5eeb1538b53d25d3cf781b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779145999</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Large Language Model (LLM)">
      <data key="d0">Large Language Model (LLM)</data>
      <data key="d1">model</data>
      <data key="d2">A Large Language Model (LLM) was used to analyze the 5,300 reports from the AI Incident Database with high accuracy (98%).&lt;SEP&gt;The Large Language Model is a type of AI model used in A2DEPT for algorithmic design and generation.&lt;SEP&gt;A large language model is a type of artificial intelligence model that can process and generate human-like text, used for various applications including refinement quality evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Large Language Model (LLM) is a type of model that is used in evidence-based misinformation detection pipelines.&lt;SEP&gt;Existing multi-agent LLM frameworks for code generation use execution feedback and improve iteratively using I/O test cases.&lt;SEP&gt;An LLM is a type of artificial intelligence designed to process and generate human-like language, which can be influenced by peer model identities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Large Language Model is a type of artificial intelligence model that relies on high-quality data for its capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-381164f8e5eeb1538b53d25d3cf781b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626cc6bade27470185f730dbb83ee93f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d0cda25c660336dba17f393f96c07ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c40b5f838241ec3077fe708b5fb65c5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e559bc1a568b70c4f4f5ba308022bb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e362b1abd07592b478809f67c6a95bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-45150d1184b48644574f184fced8240b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779201422</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Identity Categories">
      <data key="d0">Identity Categories</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Identity categories include class, skin tone, age, and political identity, among others.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-381164f8e5eeb1538b53d25d3cf781b5</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779146003</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AI Harms">
      <data key="d0">AI Harms</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-381164f8e5eeb1538b53d25d3cf781b5</data>
      <data key="d2">Age and political identity appear in documented AI harms at rates comparable to race and gender.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779146009</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LLM Agents">
      <data key="d0">LLM Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">LLM agents are transitioning to autonomous digital coworkers, requiring deterministic goal-directedness in conversations.&lt;SEP&gt;LLM agents rely on reusable skills that combine instructions, control flow, constraints, and tool calls.&lt;SEP&gt;LLM Agents are powerful approaches for solving dynamic and multi-step tasks using large language models.&lt;SEP&gt;LLM Agents are language models that can be vulnerable to skill poisoning attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;LLM agents are used in the study to reproduce empirical social science results with access to both data and code, as well as to reproduce results given only a paper's methods description and original data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fde07fa1707d44e8e8acbe99adf1542f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37afe8d903ca0c8a5ee1c7eea2b70a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce939ac16237a67e4084c40760f08ec3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f1b8d88def5ad634b9cb09c9574686b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00d7716eae39c6d5a5594bf34114050e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779212834</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Attention Latch">
      <data key="d0">Attention Latch</data>
      <data key="d1">phenomenon</data>
      <data key="d2">The Attention Latch is a systemic failure mode where cumulative probabilistic weight overrides mid-task updates, causing agents to remain anchored to obsolete constraints.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fde07fa1707d44e8e8acbe99adf1542f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779146208</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Information Over-squashing">
      <data key="d0">Information Over-squashing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Information Over-squashing occurs when the cumulative probabilistic weight of historical context overrides mid-task updates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fde07fa1707d44e8e8acbe99adf1542f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779146216</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Self-Synthesizing Reasoning Protocols (SSRP)">
      <data key="d0">Self-Synthesizing Reasoning Protocols (SSRP)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">SSRP is a metacognitive framework that implements a discrete separation between high-level architectural planning and turn-by-turn procedural execution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fde07fa1707d44e8e8acbe99adf1542f</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
