      <data key="d0">Discrete Autoregressive (AR) Spoken Language Models</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Discrete autoregressive (AR) SLMs are a type of SLM that discretize continuous speech for better performance but have high computational and data demands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46ffaa95d62e06c5c32f7e8193bede58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Continuous Diffusion (CD) Spoken Language Models">
      <data key="d0">Continuous Diffusion (CD) Spoken Language Models</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Continuous diffusion (CD) SLMs are a type of SLM that may be more viable than AR SLMs due to lower computational and data demands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46ffaa95d62e06c5c32f7e8193bede58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148638</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Phoneme Jensen-Shannon Divergence (pJSD) Metric">
      <data key="d0">Phoneme Jensen-Shannon Divergence (pJSD) Metric</data>
      <data key="d1">metric</data>
      <data key="d2">The phoneme Jensen-Shannon divergence (pJSD) metric is a measure of the linguistic quality of SLMs, introduced in this research paper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46ffaa95d62e06c5c32f7e8193bede58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Scaling Laws for Validation Loss and pJSD">
      <data key="d0">Scaling Laws for Validation Loss and pJSD</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Scaling laws refer to the relationship between model size and performance, which CD SLMs exhibit for validation loss and pJSD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46ffaa95d62e06c5c32f7e8193bede58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148643</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Token-to-Parameter Ratios">
      <data key="d0">Token-to-Parameter Ratios</data>
      <data key="d1">metric</data>
      <data key="d2">The optimal token-to-parameter ratios decrease as compute scales, indicating that larger models are more efficient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46ffaa95d62e06c5c32f7e8193bede58</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148647</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="All That Glitters Is Not Audio: Rethinking Text Priors and Audio Reliance in Audio-Language Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">All That Glitters Is Not Audio: Rethinking Text Priors and Audio Reliance in Audio-Language Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">article</data>
      <data key="d2">The article "All That Glitters Is Not Audio" discusses the limitations of current audio-language evaluation methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd399b744aa24b4b8e4a44b747320f02</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148816</data>
