      <data key="d0">Large Audio-Language Models</data>
      <data key="d1">model</data>
      <data key="d2">Large Audio-Language Models are a type of artificial intelligence that show consistent performance gains across speech and audio benchmarks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd399b744aa24b4b8e4a44b747320f02</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148816</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Speech and Audio Benchmarks">
      <data key="d0">Speech and Audio Benchmarks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Speech and Audio Benchmarks are evaluation metrics used to assess the performance of Large Audio-Language Models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd399b744aa24b4b8e4a44b747320f02</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148823</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Text Prior">
      <data key="d0">Text Prior</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Text Prior measures answerability from text and general knowledge alone, without processing the acoustic signal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd399b744aa24b4b8e4a44b747320f02</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148823</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Audio Reliance">
      <data key="d0">Audio Reliance</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Audio Reliance assesses actual dependency on the acoustic signal in audio-language evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd399b744aa24b4b8e4a44b747320f02</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148827</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Framework">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Framework</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The diagnostic framework uses two axes: text prior and audio reliance to evaluate Large Audio-Language Models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd399b744aa24b4b8e4a44b747320f02</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148827</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Global Context or Local Detail? Adaptive Visual Grounding for Hallucination Mitigation">
      <data key="d0">Global Context or Local Detail? Adaptive Visual Grounding for Hallucination Mitigation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The paper discusses a method to mitigate object hallucinations in Vision-Language Models (VLMs) by enforcing visual fidelity through Positive-and-Negative Decoding (PND).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b83f3702bd088008cc6feaedfe42bcc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779148997</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Vision-Language Models (VLMs)">
      <data key="d0">Vision-Language Models (VLMs)</data>
      <data key="d1">model</data>
      <data key="d2">Vision-Language Models are frequently undermined by object hallucination due to an over-reliance on linguistic priors.&lt;SEP&gt;Vision-Language Models (VLMs) are a type of artificial intelligence model that combines vision and language capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Vision-language models enable joint reasoning over text and images but can be brittle for end-to-end prompting.&lt;SEP&gt;Vision-Language Models are a type of multimodal model that combines computer vision and natural language processing for tasks such as image captioning and visual question answering.&lt;SEP&gt;Vision-language models are used to generate short textual rationales for model predictions in diabetic retinopathy grading.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b83f3702bd088008cc6feaedfe42bcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee6a8292ee988e4d64c218b75e3c5f57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a01dcb477b76a4cacbd754ce4561d7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da21e03ceeb42171cedd9a558d6bcf3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-944fe7c5b62b63d7ff6de501644a34a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779192278</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Positive-and-Negative Decoding (PND)">
      <data key="d0">Positive-and-Negative Decoding (PND)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">PND is a training-free inference framework that intervenes directly in the decoding process to enforce visual fidelity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b83f3702bd088008cc6feaedfe42bcc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779149004</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Object Hallucination">
      <data key="d0">Object Hallucination</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Object hallucination refers to generating content that contradicts visual reality due to an over-reliance on linguistic priors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b83f3702bd088008cc6feaedfe42bcc</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779149004</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="POPE, MME, and CHAIR">
      <data key="d0">POPE, MME, and CHAIR</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">The paper evaluates PND on benchmarks like POPE, MME, and CHAIR.</data>
