    <node id="QED">
      <data key="d0">QED</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">QED is an open-source multi-agent system for generating mathematical proofs on open problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160712</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Research-Level Proof Tasks">
      <data key="d0">Research-Level Proof Tasks</data>
      <data key="d1">task</data>
      <data key="d2">Research-level proof tasks involve generating original and nontrivial mathematical proofs for open research problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Context Contamination">
      <data key="d0">Context Contamination</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Context contamination is a failure mode that prevents reliable proof generation in LLMs, where the model's output is influenced by irrelevant information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160719</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Citation Hallucination">
      <data key="d0">Citation Hallucination</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Citation hallucination is a failure mode where LLMs incorrectly cite sources or fabricate references to support their proofs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hand-Waving on Key Steps">
      <data key="d0">Hand-Waving on Key Steps</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hand-waving on key steps refers to the tendency of LLMs to gloss over critical parts of the proof, rather than providing a detailed and rigorous explanation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160723</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Misallocation of Proof Effort">
      <data key="d0">Misallocation of Proof Effort</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Misallocation of proof effort occurs when LLMs focus too much time and resources on trivial aspects of the problem, neglecting more important details.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Unstable Proof Plans">
      <data key="d0">Unstable Proof Plans</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Unstable proof plans refer to the tendency of LLMs to generate multiple, conflicting proof strategies for a single problem.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160728</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Unfocused Verification">
      <data key="d0">Unfocused Verification</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Unfocused verification occurs when LLMs fail to thoroughly verify their own proofs, leading to potential errors and inaccuracies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Problem Modification">
      <data key="d0">Problem Modification</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Problem modification refers to the tendency of LLMs to alter or modify the original problem statement in order to make it easier to solve.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160732</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Single-Model Bottleneck">
      <data key="d0">Single-Model Bottleneck</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Single-model bottleneck occurs when a single LLM is unable to generate a proof for a particular problem, despite its strong benchmark performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-534eeaf4b91a65225ef7c1e0da500b8b</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779160736</data>
