    <node id="Antidistillation Methods">
      <data key="d0">Antidistillation Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Antidistillation methods aim to poison reasoning traces to hinder downstream student model learning while maintaining teacher performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a260fdd44522539c1799dc8fb6c8a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188192</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="TraceGuard">
      <data key="d0">TraceGuard</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TraceGuard is a post-generation black-box method that poisons sentences with high importance for teacher reasoning, developed as part of this research work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a260fdd44522539c1799dc8fb6c8a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188196</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Stackelberg Game">
      <data key="d0">Stackelberg Game</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">A Stackelberg game is a theoretical formulation used to ground antidistillation methods and reveal desired design properties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0a260fdd44522539c1799dc8fb6c8a3</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188196</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="SemBugger">
      <data key="d0">SemBugger</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">SemBugger is a polymorphic Semantic Communication backdoor that dynamically adjusts trigger intensity to generate diverse malicious results from the system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d8e68a03ba9cc110c9eba2a3ce0a75e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188334</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Semantic Communication (SC)">
      <data key="d0">Semantic Communication (SC)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Semantic Communication refers to a method of communication where systems produce predetermined outputs via shared knowledge, vulnerable to backdoor attacks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d8e68a03ba9cc110c9eba2a3ce0a75e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188334</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Trigger Intensity">
      <data key="d0">Trigger Intensity</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Trigger intensity is a measure used by SemBugger to finely-grained control over SC knowledge and generate diverse malicious results from the system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d8e68a03ba9cc110c9eba2a3ce0a75e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188341</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Effect Poisoning-Training Framework">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Effect Poisoning-Training Framework</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The multi-effect poisoning-training framework is a method used in SemBugger to introduce graded-intensity triggers, poison training data, and optimize SC systems with hierarchical malicious loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d8e68a03ba9cc110c9eba2a3ce0a75e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188341</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Hierarchical Malicious Loss">
      <data key="d0">Hierarchical Malicious Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Hierarchical malicious loss refers to the optimization of SC systems using a multi-effect poisoning-training framework that introduces graded-intensity triggers and optimizes systems with hierarchical malicious loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d8e68a03ba9cc110c9eba2a3ce0a75e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188345</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Provable Robustness Defense">
      <data key="d0">Provable Robustness Defense</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The provable robustness defense is a method proposed to resist SemBugger's homogeneous attacks through a controlled noise mechanism, adding noise in SC inputs to neutralize SemBugger attacks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d8e68a03ba9cc110c9eba2a3ce0a75e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188345</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="EPO-Safe">
      <data key="d0">EPO-Safe</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">EPO-Safe is a framework for large language model agents to discover hidden safety objectives through experience alone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-686d8a138b031e7ea18550cf04496266</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188494</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LLM">
      <data key="d0">LLM</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The LLM is the core component of EPO-Safe, responsible for generating action plans and evolving natural language behavioral specifications.&lt;SEP&gt;LLMs are large language models that can be improved by incorporating explicit belief graphs for cooperative multi-agent reasoning.&lt;SEP&gt;LLM (Large Language Model) agents operate in open-ended environments but remain largely stateless, solving each task from scratch without converting past experience into better future behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-686d8a138b031e7ea18550cf04496266&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324dd0cf578b6ecac0e07251402882b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6493a88a5a5c0fd31a6f09528518bb40</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779217966</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AI Safety Gridworlds">
      <data key="d0">AI Safety Gridworlds</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">AI Safety Gridworlds are a set of environments used to evaluate the safety performance of agents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-686d8a138b031e7ea18550cf04496266</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188501</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Text-Based Scenario Analogues">
      <data key="d0">Text-Based Scenario Analogues</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">Text-based scenario analogues are environments where visible reward may diverge from the hidden performance function $R^*$.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-686d8a138b031e7ea18550cf04496266</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188501</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="$R^*$">
      <data key="d0">$R^*$</data>
      <data key="d1">metric</data>
      <data key="d2">$R^*$ is the hidden performance function that agents aim to optimize for safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-686d8a138b031e7ea18550cf04496266</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="EPO-Safe Framework">
      <data key="d0">EPO-Safe Framework</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The EPO-Safe framework consists of three main components: iterative action plan generation, sparse binary danger warnings, and reflection-based specification evolution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-686d8a138b031e7ea18550cf04496266</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188505</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Constitutional AI">
      <data key="d0">Constitutional AI</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-686d8a138b031e7ea18550cf04496266</data>
      <data key="d2">Constitutional AI and EPO-Safe are two distinct approaches to safety specification: Constitutional AI relies on human-authored rules, while EPO-Safe discovers specifications autonomously through interaction.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188516</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reward-Driven Reflection">
      <data key="d0">Reward-Driven Reflection</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-686d8a138b031e7ea18550cf04496266</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard reward-driven reflection degrades safety by justifying and accelerating reward hacking.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188520</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="StoryTR">
      <data key="d0">StoryTR</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">StoryTR is a video moment retrieval benchmark requiring Theory of Mind (ToM) reasoning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82847022815e5b9987671137445789a4</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779188631</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Theory of Mind (ToM)">
      <data key="d0">Theory of Mind (ToM)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Theory of Mind (ToM) refers to the cognitive ability to infer implicit intentions, mental states, and narrative causality from surface-level observations.&lt;SEP&gt;Theory of Mind refers to the ability of an agent to understand and reason about other agents' mental states, which is essential in cooperative multi-agent reasoning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82847022815e5b9987671137445789a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-324dd0cf578b6ecac0e07251402882b4</data>
