      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="ASP(Q)">
      <data key="d0">ASP(Q)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">ASP(Q) is an extension of answer set programming that handles inconsistent prioritized data by exploiting a priority relation between conflicting facts to define optimal repairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4ffff9ba5dac98a07aac47ac8e0de46</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220294</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Prioritized Data">
      <data key="d0">Prioritized Data</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">Prioritized data refers to information where conflicting facts are associated with priority relations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4ffff9ba5dac98a07aac47ac8e0de46</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220301</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Optimal Repairs">
      <data key="d0">Optimal Repairs</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Optimal repairs refer to the three notions of optimal solutions (Pareto-, globally- and completion-optimal) that can be derived from prioritized data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4ffff9ba5dac98a07aac47ac8e0de46</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220301</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="AR Semantics">
      <data key="d0">AR Semantics</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">AR semantics is a variant of well-known semantics that uses Pareto-optimal repairs for query answering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4ffff9ba5dac98a07aac47ac8e0de46</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220305</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Brave Semantics">
      <data key="d0">Brave Semantics</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Brave semantics is another variant of well-known semantics that uses globally-optimal repairs for query answering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4ffff9ba5dac98a07aac47ac8e0de46</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220305</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="IAR Semantics">
      <data key="d0">IAR Semantics</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">IAR semantics is a third variant of well-known semantics that also uses globally-optimal repairs for query answering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4ffff9ba5dac98a07aac47ac8e0de46</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220309</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Globally-Optimal Repair-Based Semantics">
      <data key="d0">Globally-Optimal Repair-Based Semantics</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Globally-optimal repair-based semantics is an implementation of optimal repair-based semantics, which has been presented in this paper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4ffff9ba5dac98a07aac47ac8e0de46</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220309</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Grounded Semantics">
      <data key="d0">Grounded Semantics</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Grounded semantics is a tractable under-approximation of all optimal repair-based semantics and has been implemented for the first time in this paper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4ffff9ba5dac98a07aac47ac8e0de46</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220314</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Reservoir Computing">
      <data key="d0">Reservoir Computing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Reservoir computing is an emerging recurrent neural network architecture with low training cost and modest hardware requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a46b0909fa6d0ef55b6d73270c41134</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220468</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Memristor-Based Circuits">
      <data key="d0">Memristor-Based Circuits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Memristor-based circuits are particularly promising for reservoir computing due to their intrinsic dynamics reducing network size and parameter overhead.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a46b0909fa6d0ef55b6d73270c41134</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220468</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Parallel Delayed Feedback Network (PDFN)">
      <data key="d0">Parallel Delayed Feedback Network (PDFN)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">The parallel delayed feedback network is a reservoir computing architecture with volatile memristors, analyzed in this paper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a46b0909fa6d0ef55b6d73270c41134</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220475</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Memristive Devices">
      <data key="d0">Memristive Devices</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">Memristive devices are used in reservoir computing to reduce network size and parameter overhead due to their intrinsic dynamics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a46b0909fa6d0ef55b6d73270c41134</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220479</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Neuromorphic Computing Systems">
      <data key="d0">Neuromorphic Computing Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuromorphic computing systems are compact, high-speed, and energy-efficient systems that can be built using volatile memristors as key building blocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a46b0909fa6d0ef55b6d73270c41134</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220479</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DryRUN">
      <data key="d0">DryRUN</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">DryRUN is a framework that eliminates the need for ground-truth samples by allowing the LLM to iteratively plan, autonomously generate its own inputs and simulate execution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2f0b107ac5607c2f522733e662e18e6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220587</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LiveCodeBench v6 dataset">
      <data key="d0">LiveCodeBench v6 dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">The LiveCodeBench v6 dataset is used in evaluations of DryRUN's performance against CodeSIM.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2f0b107ac5607c2f522733e662e18e6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220587</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CodeSIM">
      <data key="d0">CodeSIM</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">CodeSIM is a state-of-the-art framework that relies on public test cases and external execution feedback for code generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2f0b107ac5607c2f522733e662e18e6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LLM-Driven Code Generation">
      <data key="d0">LLM-Driven Code Generation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">LLM-driven code generation involves using large language models to generate functionally correct code.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2f0b107ac5607c2f522733e662e18e6</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220594</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="CoFEE">
      <data key="d0">CoFEE</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">CoFEE is a framework for enforcing cognitive behaviors in Large Language Models (LLMs) to improve feature discovery from complex unstructured data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d12d8e8dc772ca87c592faaba5ae48e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220751</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Cognitive Feature Engineering Engine (CoFEE)">
      <data key="d0">Cognitive Feature Engineering Engine (CoFEE)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">CoFEE is a framework that enforces cognitive behaviors in LLMs to improve feature discovery by inducing structured inductive biases over candidate features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d12d8e8dc772ca87c592faaba5ae48e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Backward Chaining from Outcomes">
      <data key="d0">Backward Chaining from Outcomes</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Backward chaining from outcomes is one of the cognitive behaviors exploited in CoFEE, which involves reasoning backward from a target outcome to identify relevant features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d12d8e8dc772ca87c592faaba5ae48e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220758</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Subgoal Decomposition">
      <data key="d0">Subgoal Decomposition</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Subgoal decomposition is another cognitive behavior used in CoFEE, where complex tasks are broken down into smaller subgoals to improve feature discovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d12d8e8dc772ca87c592faaba5ae48e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220763</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Verification against Observability and Leakage Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Verification against Observability and Leakage Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Verification against observability and leakage criteria is a cognitive behavior that ensures features are not based on post-outcome signals or proxies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d12d8e8dc772ca87c592faaba5ae48e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220763</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Explicit Backtracking of Rejected Reasoning Paths">
      <data key="d0">Explicit Backtracking of Rejected Reasoning Paths</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Explicit backtracking of rejected reasoning paths is a cognitive behavior used in CoFEE to improve feature discovery by considering alternative explanations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d12d8e8dc772ca87c592faaba5ae48e</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220767</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Success Rate Score">
      <data key="d0">Success Rate Score</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d12d8e8dc772ca87c592faaba5ae48e</data>
      <data key="d2">CoFEE achieves an average Success Rate Score that is 15.2% higher than the vanilla approach.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220773</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Feature Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Feature Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d12d8e8dc772ca87c592faaba5ae48e</data>
      <data key="d2">CoFEE's cognitively induced features generalize beyond the data used for discovery and show improvements in quality and efficiency of LLM-based feature discovery.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220780</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Metamorphic Testing Approach">
      <data key="d0">Metamorphic Testing Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The Metamorphic Testing Approach is a method used to diagnose memorization in LLM-Based Program Repair by combining metamorphic testing with negative log-likelihood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0165fe04be31ce6f032cb41873f78f7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220922</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="LLM-Based Automated Program Repair">
      <data key="d0">LLM-Based Automated Program Repair</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">LLM-Based Automated Program Repair is an approach that uses large language models to automatically repair programs, reducing debugging costs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0165fe04be31ce6f032cb41873f78f7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220922</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Data Leakage">
      <data key="d0">Data Leakage</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Data leakage refers to the phenomenon where LLMs memorize bug fixes when evaluation benchmarks overlap with their pretraining data, leading to inflated performance estimates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0165fe04be31ce6f032cb41873f78f7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220929</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Defects4J and GitBug-Java Datasets">
      <data key="d0">Defects4J and GitBug-Java Datasets</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">The Defects4J and GitBug-Java datasets are widely used benchmarking tools for evaluating the effectiveness of program repair techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0165fe04be31ce6f032cb41873f78f7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220929</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="GPT-4o, Llama-3.1, and other LLMs">
      <data key="d0">GPT-4o, Llama-3.1, and other LLMs</data>
      <data key="d1">model</data>
      <data key="d2">GPT-4o, Llama-3.1, and other large language models are evaluated in this study to assess their performance on original and transformed benchmarks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0165fe04be31ce6f032cb41873f78f7</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779220933</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
