    <node id="Understanding and Mitigating Spurious Signal Amplification in Test-Time Reinforcement Learning for Math Reasoning">
      <data key="d0">Understanding and Mitigating Spurious Signal Amplification in Test-Time Reinforcement Learning for Math Reasoning</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">The paper focuses on addressing the issue of spurious signal amplification in test-time reinforcement learning for math reasoning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ad0b77a42149394d78abd3785c28b27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779227874</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Test-time Reinforcement Learning (TTRL)">
      <data key="d0">Test-time Reinforcement Learning (TTRL)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">TTRL is a technique that adapts models at inference time via pseudo-labeling, making it vulnerable to spurious optimization signals from label noise.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ad0b77a42149394d78abd3785c28b27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779227874</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Spurious Signal Amplification">
      <data key="d0">Spurious Signal Amplification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Spurious signal amplification refers to the phenomenon where responses with medium consistency form an ambiguity region and constitute the primary source of reward noise.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ad0b77a42149394d78abd3785c28b27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779227881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Debiased and Denoised test-time Reinforcement Learning (DDRL)">
      <data key="d0">Debiased and Denoised test-time Reinforcement Learning (DDRL)</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">DDRL is a unified framework proposed to mitigate spurious signals, which consists of three stages: frequency-based sampling, debiased advantage estimation, and consensus-based off-policy refinement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ad0b77a42149394d78abd3785c28b27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779227881</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Frequency-Based Sampling Strategy">
      <data key="d0">Frequency-Based Sampling Strategy</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This strategy excludes ambiguous samples while maintaining a balanced set of positive and negative examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ad0b77a42149394d78abd3785c28b27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779227886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Debiased Advantage Estimation with Fixed Advantages">
      <data key="d0">Debiased Advantage Estimation with Fixed Advantages</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This stage removes the bias introduced by group-relative policy optimization using fixed advantages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ad0b77a42149394d78abd3785c28b27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779227886</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Consensus-Based Off-Policy Refinement Stage">
      <data key="d0">Consensus-Based Off-Policy Refinement Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">This stage leverages the rejection-sampled dataset to enable efficient and stable model updates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ad0b77a42149394d78abd3785c28b27</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779227890</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="MiMIC">
      <data key="d0">MiMIC</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">MiMIC is a method for mitigating visual modality collapse in universal multimodal retrieval while avoiding semantic misalignment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6bcf86f6a5a967b301b93060c890f68</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779228042</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Universal Multimodal Retrieval (UMR)">
      <data key="d0">Universal Multimodal Retrieval (UMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">UMR aims to map different modalities into a shared embedding space for multi-modal retrieval.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6bcf86f6a5a967b301b93060c890f68</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779228042</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Marvel">
      <data key="d0">Marvel</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">Marvel is an early-fusion approach that projects visual features into the language model (LM) space for integrating with text modality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6bcf86f6a5a967b301b93060c890f68</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779228049</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="UniVL-DR">
      <data key="d0">UniVL-DR</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d2">UniVL-DR is a late-fusion approach that encodes visual and textual inputs using separate encoders and obtains fused embeddings through addition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6bcf86f6a5a967b301b93060c890f68</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1779228049</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="WebQA+">
      <data key="d0">WebQA+</data>
      <data key="d1">dataset</data>
      <data key="d2">WebQA+ is a dataset used for evaluating multimodal retrieval methods, where image in documents or queries might lack captions.</data>
